PLACES=[
N('batsto-village','Batsto Village','Washington Township','njs','town','standing',39.64339,-74.64819,'site','open','walk','','1766–1876',
  'Charles Read built the Batsto Iron Works and its furnace on the Batsto River by the end of 1766, smelting the bog ore dug from Pine Barrens streams with charcoal. Journey Through Jersey says the furnace sent iron goods, cannon and cannonballs among them, to the Continental Army, and that the village later made window glass and street lamps. The Philadelphia industrialist Joseph Wharton bought it in 1876 and ran it as a farming, cranberry and sawmill estate; the state bought the Wharton lands in the 1950s, and the last resident left in 1989.',
  'More than forty buildings and structures (Wikipedia; the Pinelands Alliance says thirty-three): the Wharton mansion (Journey Through Jersey calls it 36 rooms, Wikipedia 50), an 1882 water-powered sawmill, a gristmill, a general store, workers\' houses, a charcoal kiln, ice and milk houses, a carriage house and stable, a blacksmith and wheelwright shop, a 19th-century ore boat, and an 1852 post office still open, which hand-cancels mail with no ZIP code. The mansion is seen by guided tour only; interior not otherwise checked.',
  'An iron town that cast for Washington, kept as Wharton left it.',
  'Wharton State Forest, New Jersey Division of Parks and Forestry, 31 Batsto Road, Hammonton. Hours as Journey Through Jersey gives them (the state\'s own page would not open to the tools). Parking fee on weekends from Memorial Day to Labor Day: $5 for New Jersey residents, $7 for others. Visitor center with restrooms and a nature center.',
  [W('Batsto Village, New Jersey'),JTJ_BATSTO,PA_GHOST,HM(307153,'A Pane in the Glass Factory (Batsto)')],
  th=[39.64482,-74.64698],thn='Batsto Village lot (Wharton State Forest)',brick='?',
  hours='Grounds dawn to dusk daily; visitor center 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. daily; mansion by guided tour, times by phone'),
N('atsion-mansion','Atsion Mansion and village','Shamong Township','njs','landmark','standing',39.74176,-74.72549,'exact','open','walk','','1765–1826',
  'Charles Read set up an iron forge at Atsion in 1765–66; three Philadelphia Quakers added a furnace in 1774, and the forge made camp kettles for the Continental Army. The ironmaster Samuel Richards bought the works in 1824 and in 1826 built the mansion as a summer house, with a store in 1827 and a church on land he deeded in 1826. After his death in 1842 the iron trade faded; a paper mill, later a cotton mill, and a planned farm town called Fruitland followed, then Joseph Wharton bought Atsion in 1892 and used the mansion to pack and store cranberries. The state bought it in the 1950s.',
  'The Greek Revival mansion, restored to its 1826 look and never fitted with plumbing or electricity. The marker says its porch columns are cast-iron water pipes, probably from Richards\'s Weymouth Furnace, and that each of its 14 rooms has its own fireplace, with an eight-foot cooking hearth in the cellar. The church and its cemetery stand on Quaker Bridge Road nearby (HMdb marker). The forges and mills are gone.',
  'An ironmaster\'s summer house with water pipes for porch columns.',
  'Wharton State Forest, New Jersey Division of Parks and Forestry; forest office, parking and kiosk at Atsion on U.S. Route 206 (Wikipedia). Hours and fees as Journey Through Jersey gives them: guided tours $3 from age 12, $1 for ages 5 to 11; call the Atsion office for tour times.',
  [W('Atsion, New Jersey'),JTJ_ATSION,HM(79531,'Atsion Mansion'),HM(42338,'The Story of Atsion'),HM(79514,'Atsion Church & Cemetery'),PA_GHOST],
  th=[39.73926,-74.72938],thn='Atsion lot (Wharton State Forest)',brick='?',
  hours='Grounds dawn to dusk daily; Atsion office 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. daily; mansion tours most weekends, times by phone'),
N('martha-furnace-site','Martha Furnace site','Washington Township','njs','industry','traces',39.6828,-74.5109,'area','public','hike','','1793–1840s',
  'Isaac Potts of Pennsylvania built the furnace on the Oswego River in 1793, naming it for his wife, and it went into blast that September; for most of its life it was owned by Samuel Richards and run by Jesse Evans, casting goods and pig iron from bog ore with charcoal and shell flux. The Pinelands Alliance lists a stamping mill, sawmill, gristmill, store, school, hospital, ironmaster\'s house and about fifty houses. Ironmaking stopped in the mid-1840s and the village was abandoned; Joseph Wharton bought the land in 1896, and it came to the state in 1954.',
  'Little to see: after the state archaeologist Budd Wilson studied the furnace in 1968, its stack was covered with sand and fenced in the 1970s against vandalism (Wikipedia; the Pinelands Alliance also says buried and fenced). The Batona Trail passes the site and crosses the Oswego on an old road bridge just below the dam. Leave the mound alone.',
  'An iron town of fifty houses now under sand and pines.',
  'Wharton State Forest, New Jersey Division of Parks and Forestry; reached on the Batona Trail or the sand Martha Road (Wikipedia, Pinelands Alliance). No mapped lot; the forest roads are unpaved. Forest hours and rules not checked: the state\'s page would not open.',
  [W('Martha Furnace (New Jersey)'),PA_GHOST],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('hampton-furnace-ruins','Hampton Furnace ruins','Shamong Township','njs','industry','ruins',39.7705,-74.67926,'site','public','hike','','late 18th century',
  'The Pinelands Alliance lists Hampton Furnace among the easier Pine Barrens ghost sites to reach: the ruins of an iron furnace of the late 1700s and 1800s on the Batsto River, later part of a cranberry operation.',
  'OpenStreetMap maps a ruined building at the pin. What survives of the furnace itself, and which ruins belong to the cranberry years, was not checked.',
  'An iron furnace that became a cranberry farm.',
  'Wharton State Forest, New Jersey Division of Parks and Forestry (Pinelands Alliance). Reached by unpaved forest roads; no mapped lot. Forest hours and rules not checked.',
  [PA_GHOST],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('friendship-cranberry-ruins','Friendship cranberry village ruins','Washington Township','njs','town','ruins',39.75084,-74.58428,'site','public','walk','','1869',
  'In 1869 Friendship was the centre of a 3,000-acre cranberry farm, a small company town in the Pine Barrens that was later abandoned (Pinelands Alliance).',
  'Foundation ruins in the field on the east side of Carranza Road and the south side of Friendship-Speedwell Road, with a view of meadow, woods and a cedar stream. The exact spot of each foundation was not checked.',
  'A cranberry company town gone back to field.',
  'Wharton State Forest, New Jersey Division of Parks and Forestry (Pinelands Alliance). Carranza Road and Friendship-Speedwell Road are unpaved forest roads. Forest hours and rules not checked.',
  [PA_GHOST,HM(244465,'Speedwell (nearby, on Friendship-Speedwell Road)')],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('whitesbog-village','Whitesbog Village','Pemberton Township','njs','town','standing',39.95966,-74.50974,'site','open','walk','','1857–1925',
  'James Fenwick began growing cranberries here in 1857; his son-in-law Joseph J. White took over in 1882, and a company town grew up, with two outlying villages, Rome and Florence, out in the bogs; at its peak about 600 people worked here. In 1916 White\'s daughter Elizabeth Coleman White and Frederick Coville of the US Department of Agriculture produced the first commercial crop of cultivated highbush blueberries here. The state bought the land in the 1960s for what is now Brendan T. Byrne State Forest.',
  'The marker says every building in the village went up between 1890 and 1925, and 21 were restored between 2004 and 2010. The 1924 general store, the long white Cranberry & Blueberry Museum building, and Elizabeth White\'s house, Suningive (1923), in its native-plant garden. Volunteers open buildings on Saturdays; trails run out among the bogs.',
  'The birthplace of the cultivated blueberry, still among its bogs.',
  'Owned by the State of New Jersey (Brendan T. Byrne State Forest) and leased to the Whitesbog Preservation Trust, 120 W. Whites Bog Road, Browns Mills. Main lot holds about 80 cars, with more behind the museum building (Trust FAQ). Blueberry picking in season for a $5 donation.',
  [WB_VISIT,WB_FAQ,HM(35117,'Whitesbog Village'),HM(34274,'Elizabeth Coleman White'),W('Brendan T. Byrne State Forest')],
  brick='?',
  hours='Grounds and trails sunrise to sunset daily; general store and open buildings Saturdays 10 a.m. to 2 p.m.'),
N('burlington-county-prison-museum','Burlington County Prison','Mount Holly','njs','inst','standing',39.99659,-74.78926,'exact','open','drive','','1810–1965',
  'Robert Mills designed the county prison; building began in 1810 and the first prisoners came in 1811. It was planned on reform lines, with single cells, good air and fireproof masonry, and over the door is Mills\'s motto, "Justice which, while it punishes, would endeavor to reform the offender". It held prisoners until 1965 and is now a museum and a National Historic Landmark.',
  'A three-storey U-shaped building of dressed stone under a hip roof, with a heavy wooden door on strap hinges. Inside, brick-lined passages, cells with wooden floors, larger debtors\' cells, and on the top floor a cell for the most dangerous prisoners with its wall shackles still in place (Wikipedia, from the landmark nomination). Open to visitors at the hours below.',
  'A reform-era jail that held prisoners for 154 years.',
  'Run as a museum by Burlington County, 128 High Street, Mount Holly. Hours and fees as Journey Through Jersey gives them: adults $5, over 55 and military $3, students $2, under five free; audio tour $3. Off-street parking.',
  [W('Burlington County Prison'),JTJ_PRISON],
  th=[39.99530,-74.79223],thn='Clifton Avenue lot (check on arrival)',brick='?',
  hours='Thursday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Sunday noon to 4 p.m.'),
N('smithville-historic-park','Smithville Mansion and village','Eastampton Township','njs','town','standing',39.9867,-74.7492,'exact','open','walk','','1831–1920s',
  'Jonathan and Samuel Shreve founded a textile village here on Rancocas Creek in 1831 (a sawmill, gristmill and dam had stood on the site since 1780). Hezekiah Bradley Smith bought it in 1865 and renamed it; his H. B. Smith Machine Company made woodworking machines and, from 1880, the American Star bicycle, and in 1892 built a monorail bicycle railroad 1.8 miles to Mount Holly for its workers, which fell into disrepair by 1897. Burlington County bought the property in 1975 as its first park.',
  'The Smith mansion, shown by guided tour, with a worker\'s house, a schoolhouse, the Underground Railroad Museum of Burlington County and a visitor centre in the old village; the 312-acre park has trails and Smithville Lake. Interior of the mansion seen only by tour; other fabric not checked.',
  'A factory village that built bicycles and a bicycle railway.',
  'Historic Smithville Park, Burlington County parks, 803 Smithville Road, Eastampton. Hours as Journey Through Jersey gives them: Underground Railroad Museum and Workers House Museum Thursday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. and Sunday noon to 4 p.m.; visitor centre Tuesday to Sunday 12:30 to 4 p.m. Mansion tours $8 adults, $7 seniors and students.',
  [W('Smithville, Burlington County, New Jersey'),JTJ_SMITH],
  th=[39.98025,-74.74273],thn='East Railroad Avenue lot, Historic Smithville Park',brick='?',
  hours='Park dawn to dusk daily; mansion tours May to October, Thursday and Sunday at noon, 1 and 2 p.m.'),
N('kirbys-mill-medford','Kirby\'s Mill','Medford Township','njs','industry','standing',39.91695,-74.80577,'site','open','drive','','1778–1969',
  'Isaac Haines and partners built a gristmill here on the Southwest Branch of Rancocas Creek in 1778; a sawmill and a brick miller\'s house of about 1785 followed, and the mill was raised to three storeys about 1830. William S. Kirby bought it in 1877, added a fourth floor and replaced the water wheel with turbines. Flour stopped in the 1920s and feed in 1969; Wikipedia calls it the last commercially working mill in New Jersey. It came through the flood of July 2004 nearly undamaged.',
  'The four-storey mill, the sawmill, the miller\'s house, a blacksmith shop and barn, with a country store, milliner, print and carpenter shops as part of the Medford Historical Society\'s museum. Machinery not checked.',
  'A mill that ground feed until 1969.',
  'Owned by the Medford Historical Society, on Church Road at Kirbys Mill. Wikipedia (citing the society in 2011) and Medford Sun articles of 2013 to 2017 say the mill museum opens on Sunday afternoons in summer and for events such as the Apple Festival; the society\'s current page gives no mill hours, so check before going.',
  [W('Kirby\'s Mill'),L('Medford Historical Society','https://medfordhistorical.org/visit/')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('old-schoolhouse-mount-holly','Old Schoolhouse (Brainerd School)','Mount Holly','njs','civic','standing',39.99492,-74.78619,'exact','open','drive','','1759',
  'Twenty-one townspeople bought shares to build this one-room school in 1759; the Colonial Dames call it the oldest surviving one-room schoolhouse in New Jersey on its original foundation. After the state set up free public schools in 1848 it served as a private school and for other teaching. The name Brainerd School comes from a link to the missionary John Brainerd that the Register nomination and the 1936 survey both call mistaken.',
  'A small Georgian schoolhouse of red brick laid with dark glazed headers (Wikimedia photograph), on the south side of Brainerd Street, kept as a museum. Interior not checked.',
  'The oldest one-room school in the state.',
  'Owned by the National Society of The Colonial Dames of America in New Jersey since 1951, 35 Brainerd Street, Mount Holly. Street parking.',
  [W('Brainerd School'),PEACH,PEACH_HOME,L('Wikimedia Commons photograph (red brick with glazed headers)','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Schoolhouse.JPG')],
  brick='yes',hours='Select Sundays and community events; tours by appointment'),
N('peachfield-westampton','Peachfield','Westampton Township','njs','landmark','standing',40.03491,-74.80206,'site','open','drive','','1725',
  'Henry Burr built the house in 1725 and his son John added the west part in 1732; it stayed in the Burr family about two hundred years. A fire nearly destroyed it, and it was restored from 1931 under the architect Brognard Okie. The Colonial Dames have kept it as a house museum since 1965, and the fields round it are still farmed.',
  'A restored early country house among working fields. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the National Society of The Colonial Dames of America in New Jersey, 180 Burrs Road, Westampton. Tours by appointment only (peachfield.org).',
  [W('Peachfield'),PEACH,PEACH_HOME],
  brick='?',hours='Tours by appointment only'),
N('point-breeze-bordentown','Point Breeze (Joseph Bonaparte\'s estate)','Bordentown','njs','inst','traces',40.15554,-74.71005,'site','public','walk','','1816–1983',
  'Joseph Bonaparte, Napoleon\'s elder brother, bought this bluff over Crosswicks Creek and the Delaware in 1816 and made it the most talked-of estate in the country, with a large art collection and library; his first mansion burned in 1820 and he built a second. His grandson sold the estate in 1847. A later mansion on the site burned in 1983; the Divine Word Missionaries owned the land from 1941 until 2020, when the City of Bordentown, D&R Greenway and the state bought the last 60 acres for a park.',
  'The 1820 Gardener\'s House, the one building left from Bonaparte\'s time, reopened by D&R Greenway as its Discovery Center with a recreated garden; walking trails over the grounds. The 2023 park map marks a stone grotto and a pavilion as closed to the public.',
  'What is left of the house that rivalled the White House.',
  'Point Breeze at D&R Canal State Park: a partnership of the state, the City of Bordentown (whose municipal complex opened on 5.6 acres in 2022) and D&R Greenway, which owns the Gardener\'s House, 101 Park Street. The federal public-land map still marks the land closed (XA); the 2023 park map shows public parking, trails and fields open, with the grotto and pavilion fenced. The Discovery Center\'s hours are as D&R Greenway announced them at its opening (date of that page not checked); admission free.',
  [W('Point Breeze (estate)'),DRG_PB,DRCSP_PB,CJ_PB],
  brick='?',hours='Discovery Center Saturday and Sunday noon to 4 p.m. (as announced at its opening)'),
N('arneys-mount-meeting-house','Arney\'s Mount Friends Meetinghouse','Springfield Township','njs','worship','standing',40.00995,-74.69657,'site','view','drive','','18th century, rebuilt 1811',
  'Friends near Shreve\'s Mount (later Arney\'s Mount) asked leave to hold winter meetings here in 1743, and a meeting house was built some years later by Samuel Smith, whose initials are cut in a stone over the door. Fires in about 1800 and 1809 left little but the stone walls, and it was rebuilt by 1811. The preparative meeting was laid down in 1871, but worship has been held here, usually twice a month, since 1941.',
  'Built of bog-iron stone quarried from the hill it stands on, unusual in South Jersey where meeting houses are brick. Inside: a gallery on three sides with hand-hewn panels that lift into the attic, turned wooden pillars, benches held by hand-forged bolts, and charred timber from the fire under the corner stairs (Wikipedia, from the Register form). The burial ground is older than the house; its sandstone wall of 1860 or 1870 (sources give both) encloses only part of the graves.',
  'A meeting house of stone dug from its own hill.',
  'An active Friends meeting at Mount Holly-Juliustown Road and Pemberton-Arney\'s Mount Road. Look from the road; times of worship not checked.',
  [W('Arney\'s Mount Friends Meetinghouse and Burial Ground')],
  brick='no',conf='mixed'),
N('st-marys-church-burlington','St. Mary\'s Church, old and new','Burlington','njs','worship','standing',40.07668,-74.86246,'site','open','drive','','1703',
  'Old St. Mary\'s was built in 1703 on ground bought for a burying place in 1695, and John Talbot, a missionary of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel, became its rector in 1705: it is the oldest Episcopal parish in New Jersey. Richard Upjohn designed the new church next door, built 1846–54 on the model of St. John\'s, Shottesbrooke, in England; it is a National Historic Landmark. A workman\'s mistake set it on fire on Holy Thursday 1976, badly damaging the roof and interior.',
  'The small old church of 1703 and the large brownstone Gothic Revival church with its crossing spire, which holds eight bells cast at the Whitechapel foundry in 1865. The churchyard holds Elias Boudinot, President of the Continental Congress, and Governor Joseph Bloomfield, among many others. Interior fittings not checked.',
  'The oldest Episcopal parish in New Jersey, and Upjohn\'s copy of an English church.',
  'An active Episcopal parish, 145 West Broad Street, Burlington; parish office 609-386-0902. Service times from the parish\'s own page.',
  [W('St. Mary\'s Episcopal Church (Burlington, New Jersey)'),STMARY],
  brick='?',hours='Sunday 7:30 and 9 a.m.; Saturday 5:30 p.m.; Friday noon (Mass)'),
N('crosswicks-friends-meeting-house','Crosswicks Friends Meeting House','Crosswicks','njs','worship','standing',40.15203,-74.64709,'exact','view','drive','','1773',
  'Quakers built a meeting house here in 1692, a larger brick one in 1706, and the present brick house in 1773 (Wikipedia). On December 29, 1776 it was Colonel Silas Newcomb\'s command post before the second battle of Trenton, and in June 1778 British troops under Henry Clinton occupied it during the fight for the Crosswicks Creek bridge (South Jersey Quakers, The Liberty Trail).',
  'A cannonball still lodged in the north wall between the upper windows; nobody knows whether British or American guns fired it. The village round it is a historic district of 95 contributing buildings. Interior not checked.',
  'A Quaker meeting house with a cannonball in its wall.',
  'Crosswicks Friends Meeting, Front Street, Crosswicks (Chesterfield Township). Look from the street; times of worship not checked.',
  [W('Crosswicks, New Jersey'),LIBERTY_CW,SJQ_CW],
  brick='?'),
N('north-pemberton-station','North Pemberton Railroad Station','Pemberton Township','njs','rail','standing',39.977,-74.68127,'exact','open','drive','','1892–1969',
  'John S. Rogers built the station for the Pennsylvania Railroad in 1892 (Journey Through Jersey gives 1891 as the start of service); it carried the supplies for building Camp Dix. Passenger service to Camden ended in 1969. Pemberton Township owns it and runs it as a museum of local and railroad history and a Pinelands welcome centre; Wikipedia says the museum was reopening under the Pemberton Township Historic Trust after a lease dispute in 2012.',
  'A Queen Anne station building, with a nature path behind it. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by Pemberton Township, 3 Fort Dix Road. Hours as Journey Through Jersey gives them; free.',
  [W('North Pemberton station'),JTJ_PEMB],
  th=[39.97751,-74.68143],thn='Lot beside the station',brick='?',conf='mixed',
  hours='Sundays 10 a.m. to 3 p.m., by appointment'),
N('white-horse-inn-chatsworth','White Horse Inn (Shamong Hotel)','Chatsworth','njs','hotel','standing',39.81844,-74.53524,'site','view','drive','','1860',
  'The marker calls it the oldest landmark left in Chatsworth, dated by its architecture to about 1860, and says it was named the Shamong Hotel in 1876, before the town was renamed Chatsworth when Prince Mario Ruspoli formed a land syndicate there. It was later the home of Alfred Stevenson, manager of the Chatsworth Cranberry Association. It is on the National Register as the Shamong Hotel.',
  'A standing hotel building at Main Street (Route 532) and Railroad Avenue, beside a marker for the Central Railroad of New Jersey. Its present use was not checked.',
  'A Pine Barrens hotel from before the town had its name.',
  'Owner and use not checked. Look from Main Street.',
  [HM(176215,'The White Horse Inn'),HM(175906,'Central Railroad of New Jersey')],
  brick='?',conf='mixed'),
]
